Subject: virtio/fs: shard the handle table, build CREATE entries from the new fd

Every CREATE, OPEN and RELEASE write-locked one table of all open handles,
and every READ and WRITE read-locked it, so with ten parallel unzips the
vCPUs parked on that lock for about a quarter of their file-system time
(sample: waits in write, release and create). Split the table into 64
shards with their own locks; handles are numbered in order, so concurrent
streams use different shards. RELEASE under 8 parallel writers went from
28 to 14 us of host time.

CREATE opened the name through syncopen (two setiopolicy_np calls, an
fstat and two fcntls to undo O_NONBLOCK), then looked the name up again
for the entry: a second path resolution and stat. One open and an fstat
of the new descriptor are enough: open with O_EXCL through the
parent's pin (or its /.vol path), and build the entry from fstat of that
descriptor. A file the call creates cannot be dataless or a FIFO, so the
open needs no bounding; a name that already exists (the guest had not seen
it and did not ask for O_EXCL) still gets the bounded open. Host time per
CREATE went from 51 to 43 us.

